a minimum) and by at most \\(b\\) increasing subsequences (each begins at a maximum).  \n   By the Erd\u0151s\u2013Szekeres theorem, a permutation of length \\(n\\) that can be covered by \\(a\\) decreasing and \\(b\\) increasing subsequences satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\le a\\;b .\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b\\ge 2\\sqrt{n}.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n=2025=45^{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{tiles}(\\pi)\\ge 2\\cdot45=90 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Partition \\(\\{1,\\dots ,2025\\}\\) into \\(45\\) blocks of size \\(45\\):\n   \\[\n   B_{k}= \\{45(k-1)+1,\\dots ,45k\\},\\qquad k=1,\\dots ,45 .\n   \\]\n   Define the permutation  \n\n   \\[\n   \\pi=\n   \\bigl(45,44,\\dots ,1,\\;\n   90,89,\\dots ,46,\\;\n   \\dots ,\\;\n   2025,2024,\\dots ,1981\\bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. each block is written in decreasing order and the blocks are concatenated.\n\n   * Left\u2011to\u2011right maxima occur at the first element of each block \u2192 \\(b=45\\).  \n   * Left\u2011to\u2011right minima occur at every element of the first block \u2192 \\(a=45\\).\n\n   Consequently \\(\\operatorname{LMIN}(\\pi)=\\operatorname{LMAX}(\\pi)=45\\) and the tiling described in step\u202f3 uses exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b=45+45=90\n   \\]\n\n   rectangles.  The rectangles are disjoint, each lies on grid lines, and they cover all squares except the \\(2025\\) holes \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  Every row and every column contains precisely one uncovered unit square.\n\nSince the lower bound \\(90\\) is achieved, it is optimal.  \n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nPlace the uncovered unit squares at positions \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) where \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j):j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered cell belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\); the two sets are disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Rectangular decomposition of \\(L\\)\n\nA **left\u2011to\u2011right minimum** of \\(\\pi\\) is an index \\(i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(i)<\\pi(k)\\quad\\text{for all }k<i .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nm_{1}<m_{2}<\\dots <m_{a}\n\\]\n\nbe all minima (so \\(m_{1}=1\\)).  \nFor \\(k=1,\\dots ,a\\) set  \n\n\\[\nI_{k}= \\{m_{k},m_{k}+1,\\dots ,m_{k+1}-1\\},\n\\qquad m_{a+1}=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(k\\) consider the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_{k}= I_{k}\\times\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\pi(m_{k})-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Every* cell of \\(L\\) lies in exactly one of the \\(R_{k}\\):  \nif a row \\(i\\) belongs to \\(I_{k}\\) then \\(\\pi(i)\\ge\\pi(m_{k})\\) (by definition of a minimum), so the columns left of \\(\\pi(i)\\) are precisely \\(1,\\dots ,\\pi(m_{k})-1\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{R_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{a}\\) partitions \\(L\\).\n\nConsequently any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(a\\) rectangles (each \\(R_{k}\\) contains a cell \\((m_{k},\\pi(m_{k})-1)\\) that cannot belong to a rectangle covering any earlier row).  \nThe construction above shows that \\(a\\) rectangles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum number of rectangles covering \\(L\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{LMIN}(\\pi)=a .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Rectangular decomposition of \\(R\\)\n\nA **left\u2011to\u2011right maximum** of \\(\\pi\\) is an index \\(i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(i)>\\pi(k)\\quad\\text{for all }k<i .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nM_{1}<M_{2}<\\dots <M_{b}\n\\]\n\nbe all maxima (so \\(M_{1}=1\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nJ_{k}= \\{M_{k},M_{k}+1,\\dots ,M_{k+1}-1\\},\n\\qquad M_{b+1}=n+1 ,\n\\]\n\nand the rectangles  \n\n\\[\nS_{k}= J_{k}\\times\\{\\pi(M_{k})+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nExactly as for \\(L\\), the sets \\(\\{S_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{b}\\) partition \\(R\\); each \\(S_{k}\\) is a rectangle, and any tiling of \\(R\\) must contain at least one rectangle for each maximum.  \nThus the minimum number of rectangles covering \\(R\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{LMAX}(\\pi)=b .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Number of tiles for a given permutation\n\nSince the tiles covering \\(L\\) and those covering \\(R\\) are disjoint, the total number of tiles required for the permutation \\(\\pi\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{tiles}(\\pi)=\\operatorname{LMIN}(\\pi)+\\operatorname{LMAX}(\\pi)} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A universal lower bound\n\nLet \\(a=\\operatorname{LMIN}(\\pi)\\) and \\(b=\\operatorname{LMAX}(\\pi)\\).  \n\n* The rows can be partitioned into at most \\(a\\) decreasing subsequences: each decreasing subsequence starts at a left\u2011to\u2011right minimum and continues until the next minimum.  \n* Similarly the rows can be partitioned into at most \\(b\\) increasing subsequences, each starting at a left\u2011to\u2011right maximum.\n\nIf a sequence of length \\(n\\) can be covered by \\(a\\) decreasing and \\(b\\) increasing subsequences, the Erd\u0151s\u2013Szekeres theorem (or a simple pigeon\u2011hole argument) yields  \n\n\\[\nn\\le a\\;b .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\na+b\\ge 2\\sqrt{n}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025=45^{2}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{tiles}(\\pi)\\ge 2\\cdot45 = 90 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nPartition \\(\\{1,\\dots ,2025\\}\\) into \\(45\\) blocks of size \\(45\\):\n\n\\[\nB_{k}= \\{45(k-1)+1,\\dots ,45k\\},\\qquad k=1,\\dots ,45 .\n\\]\n\nDefine the permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\pi=\n\\bigl(45,44,\\dots ,1,\\;\n   90,89,\\dots ,46,\\;\n   \\dots ,\\;\n   2025,2024,\\dots ,1981\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n* **Left\u2011to\u2011right maxima.**  \n  The first element of each block is larger than every element of the preceding blocks, so a new maximum appears at the start of each block. Hence \\(\\operatorname{LMAX}(\\pi)=45\\).\n\n* **Left\u2011to\u2011right minima.**  \n  Inside the first block the values strictly decrease, giving a new minimum at each of its \\(45\\) entries. No later entry is smaller than the already attained minimum \\(1\\). Hence \\(\\operatorname{LMIN}(\\pi)=45\\).\n\nTherefore \\(\\text{tiles}(\\pi)=45+45=90\\).\n\nThe rectangles described in Sections\u202f1 and\u202f2 for this \\(\\pi\\) give an explicit tiling:\n\n* For each minimum \\(m_{k}\\) (\\(k=1,\\dots ,44\\)) a rectangle covering the single row \\(m_{k}\\) and columns \\(1,\\dots ,\\pi(m_{k})-1\\);  \n* One additional rectangle covering rows \\(45\\!+\\!1,\\dots ,2025\\) and columns \\(1,\\dots ,45\\);  \n* For each maximum \\(M_{k}\\) (\\(k=1,\\dots ,45\\)) a rectangle covering the rows from \\(M_{k}\\) up to the row before the next maximum and the columns \\(\\pi(M_{k})+1,\\dots ,2025\\).\n\nThese \\(90\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, each lies on grid lines, and together they cover every unit square except the \\(2025\\) holes \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  Consequently every row and every column contains exactly one uncovered unit square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Optimality\n\nWe have proved a lower bound of \\(90\\) tiles for any admissible configuration and exhibited a configuration that uses exactly \\(90\\) tiles.  Hence the lower bound is tight and the minimal possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{90}.\n\\]"
