el rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; it must consist of whole unit squares and tiles may not overlap.  \n\nDenote by \\(U\\) the set of uncovered unit squares.  \nThe condition “each row and each column has exactly one uncovered square’’ implies that  \n\n\\[\n|U|=n\n\\]\n\nand that the elements of \\(U\\) form a **permutation matrix**: there is a bijection  \n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\nThus the problem is to partition the set of all other squares,\n\\[\nB:=\\{(i,j):1\\le i,j\\le n\\}\\setminus U,\n\\]\ninto axis‑parallel rectangles (tiles) and to minimise the number of rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Upper bound – a construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j): i<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile covering the whole row \\(i\\) to the right of the diagonal.  \nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles cover all squares strictly **above** the diagonal.\n\nFor each \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nC_j^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,j):1\\le i<j\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\((j-1)\\times 1\\) tile covering the whole column \\(j\\) below the diagonal.  \nThese \\(n-1\\) rectangles cover all squares strictly **below** the diagonal.\n\nNo rectangle contains a diagonal square, the rectangles are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every square of \\(B\\).  \nHence we have tiled the board with  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles. Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nLet a tiling of \\(B\\) with \\(t\\) tiles be given.  \nFor each row \\(i\\) let the uncovered column be \\(p(i)\\).  \nIn row \\(i\\) the covered cells consist of two (possibly empty) contiguous blocks:\n\n* **Left block** \\(L_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<p(i)\\}\\);\n* **Right block** \\(R_i=\\{(i,j):p(i)<j\\le n\\}\\).\n\nA tile cannot contain the uncovered cell \\((i,p(i))\\); therefore a tile intersecting row \\(i\\) is either completely contained in \\(L_i\\) or completely contained in \\(R_i\\).  \n\nDefine a tile to be **left‑type** if it lies wholly inside the left block of every row it meets, and **right‑type** analogously.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1  \nThere are at least \\(n-1\\) left‑type tiles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nExactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\); for all other rows \\(L_i\\neq\\varnothing\\).  \nConsider any row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\). The cell \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) belongs to \\(L_i\\); let \\(T\\) be the (unique) tile covering this cell.  \nBecause \\(T\\) is contained in \\(L_i\\) for row \\(i\\), it is a left‑type tile.  \n\nAssign the tile \\(T\\) to the **highest** row (largest index) among the rows it occupies.  \nIf a left‑type tile were assigned to two different rows, it would have to contain two distinct cells \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) and \\((k,p(k)-1)\\) with \\(i\\neq k\\); but then its column interval would have to contain both columns \\(p(i)-1\\) and \\(p(k)-1\\), which forces it to also contain the uncovered column of the higher of the two rows, contradicting the definition of left‑type.  \n\nHence each left‑type tile receives at most one assignment, and every row with \\(p(i)>1\\) receives an assignment.  \nSince exactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\), we obtain at least \\(n-1\\) left‑type tiles. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2  \nThere are at least \\(n-1\\) right‑type tiles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe argument is symmetric. Exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\); for all other rows \\(R_i\\neq\\varnothing\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) consider the cell \\((i,p(i