s not included. So T does not contain uncovered cell (k,p(k)). So the argument is that T cannot contain two such cells from distinct rows because that would force a column that is too large? Let's examine the proof's reasoning: \"Because the columns of a rectangle are contiguous, T contains every column between p(i)-1 and p(k)-1. Since p(k) ≤ p(i) would make column p(k) lie between those two columns, T would also contain the uncovered cell (k,p(k)), contradicting the definition of a tile.\" This is a bit informal but plausible: If p(k) ≤ p(i), then p(k) is between p(k) and p(i) (or p(k)-1 and p(i)-1?). Actually if p(k) ≤ p(i), then p(k) ≤ p(i). The columns between p(i)-1 and p(k)-1 inclusive include p(k) if p(k) ≤ p(i)-1? Let's check: Suppose p(i) = 5, p(k) = 4. Then p(i)-1 = 4, p(k)-1 = 3. The columns between 4 and 3? That's ambiguous: If ik, they say \"contains every column between p(i)-1 and p(k)-1\". If p(i)-1 > p(k)-1, the interval includes columns from p(k)-1 up to p(i)-1. That includes column 4? Actually p(i)-1 = 4, p(k)-1 = 3. The columns between 3 and 4 inclusive are 3 and 4. Column p(k) = 4 is included. So yes, if p(k) ≤ p(i), then p(k) is at most p(i). Then p(k) is either equal to p(i) or less. If p(k) = p(i), then p(k)-1 = p(i)-1, the interval includes column p(i)-1 = p(k)-1, but p(k) = p(i) is one more than that, not included. However, if p(k) = p(i), then p(k) = p(i) > p(i)-1 = p(k)-1, so column p(k) is not between p(i)-1 and p(k)-1 (since they are equal). But the proof says \"Since p(k) ≤ p(i) would make column p(k) lie between those two columns\". If p(k) = p(i), then p(k) = p(i), column p(k) is not between p(i)-1 and p(k)-1 = p(i)-1. So the argument fails for equality case. But does equality case happen? If p(k) = p(i), then the uncovered squares are in same column for two rows, which cannot happen because p is a permutation (bijection). So p(k) cannot equal p(i) for distinct rows. So p(k) p(i). Then p(k) ≤ p(i)-1, so column p(k) is ≤ p(i)-1. Since 