umn is at most p(i)-1 (since it includes p(i)-1 and cannot exceed that because otherwise it would cover the uncovered cell (i,p(i))). Actually T could extend to the right beyond p(i)-1? No, because if T includes column p(i), then it would cover the uncovered cell (i,p(i)). So the tile's column interval must be ≤ p(i)-1. So the rightmost column of T is ≤ p(i)-1. For any other row r in the tile, we need p(r)-1 ≥ rightmost column of T. Since p(r) could be smaller than p(i) (i.e., p(r) p(i)), then p(r)-1 p(i)-1, so the tile's columns might exceed p(r)-1, violating left-type condition. However, the tile's column interval is within [some leftmost column, rightmost column ≤ p(i)-1]. If p(r) p(i), then p(r)-1 ≤ p(i)-2, so the tile's rightmost column (≤ p(i)-1) could be > p(r)-1, thus the tile would not be wholly inside L_r. That would make T not left-type. So the proof's claim that T is left-type may be flawed. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nDefinition: \"Define a tile to be left‑type if it lies inside the left block of every row it meets.\" So for tile T covering (i,p(i)-1), we need to check that for any row r that T covers, the columns of T are ≤ p(r)-1. Since T's columns are contiguous and include p(i)-1, the rightmost column of T is at least p(i)-1. Actually the tile includes p(i)-1, but could also extend leftwards. The rightmost column of T could be exactly p(i)-1 (if the tile's rightmost column is p(i)-1). It cannot extend to the right beyond p(i)-1 because that would cover the uncovered cell (i,p(i)). So the rightmost column is p(i)-1. The leftmost column could be any column ≤ p(i)-1. So the tile's column interval is [c, p(i)-1] for some c ≤ p(i)-1.\n\nNow consider any other row r that T covers. For T to be left-type, we need p(r)-1 ≥ p(i)-1 (since the tile's rightmost column is p(i)-1). That is, p(r) ≥ p(i). So for any row r in the tile, p(r) must be at least p(i). If there is a row r with p(r) p(i), then its left block L_r = {1,...,p(r)-1} does not contain column