y assume that a tile covering (i,p(i)-1) cannot intersect any row r where p(r) p(i) because then the tile would intersect the uncovered cell (r,p(r))? Let's see: If T covers rows i..r (ir) and includes column p(i)-1, then for row r, the tile's columns are [c, p(i)-1] where c ≤ p(i)-1. If p(r) p(i), then p(r) ≤ p(i)-1. The uncovered cell for row r is at column p(r). Since p(r) ≤ p(i)-1, the tile's column interval includes column p(r) only if c ≤ p(r) ≤ p(i)-1. But does the tile necessarily include column p(r)? Not necessarily; the tile's leftmost column c could be > p(r). But the tile could be placed such that its column interval is [p(i)-1, p(i)-1] (i.e., a single column). In that case, it would not include column p(r) if p(r) p(i)-1. So the tile could intersect row r without covering its uncovered cell. So the tile could intersect rows where p(r) p(i) without covering uncovered squares. So the tile might not be left-type. So the proof's claim that each tile covering (i,p(i)-1) is left-type is not necessarily true.\n\nThus Lemma 1 may be flawed. However, perhaps they define left-type differently: \"Define a tile to be left‑type if it lies inside the left block of every row it meets, and right‑type analogously.\" This is clear. So the tile covering (i,p(i)-1) might not be left-type if it also meets some row where p(r) p(i). But the proof doesn't address that. So there is a potential gap.\n\nBut maybe they assume that the tile covering (i,p(i)-1) cannot intersect any row r with p(r) p(i) because then it would intersect the uncovered cell (r,p(r)). Let's examine: Suppose tile T covers rows i..r (ir). The tile's column interval includes p(i)-1. If p(r) p(i), then p(r) ≤ p(i)-1. The uncovered cell (r,p(r)) is at column p(r). If the tile's column interval includes column p(r), then it would cover the uncovered cell, which is not allowed. So to avoid covering uncovered cell, the tile's column interval must not include column p(r). Since the tile's column interval includes 