iguous set of columns.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound: a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the holes lie on the main diagonal).  \nFor each column \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_k^{\\text{below}}=\\{(i,k)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\((n-k)\\times1\\).  \nFor each column \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_k^{\\text{above}}=\\{(i,k)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has size \\((k-1)\\times1\\).  \n\n*The rectangles are pairwise disjoint*: for a fixed column \\(k\\) the two rectangles occupy the rows below and above the hole \\((k,k)\\), respectively, and for distinct columns the rectangles lie in different columns.  \n\n*They cover all squares except the diagonal*: every square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<i\\) belongs to \\(R_j^{\\text{below}}\\); every square with \\(j>i\\) belongs to \\(R_j^{\\text{above}}\\).  \n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lower bound: any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFix a permutation \\(\\sigma\\).  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)>j\\},\n\\]\n\nthe set of squares in column \\(j\\) that lie **left** of the hole in their row.  \nLet \\(i_j\\) be the unique index with \\(\\sigma(i_j)=j+1\\); then \\((i_j,j)\\in L_j\\).\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim 1.}\\quad\\text{Any tile covering a square of }L_j\\text{ has rightmost column }j.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a tile \\(T\\) covering \\((i_j,j)\\) extended to column \\(j+1\\).  \nSince \\(\\sigma(i_j)=j+1\\), the square \\((i_j,j+1)\\) is a hole, which would lie inside \\(T\\), contradicting the rules. ∎\n\nConsequently each tile that co