(i)+1), we need p(i)+1 >= c. So p(i) >= c-1. Combined with p(i) < c, we get p(i) = c-1. So for rectangle to contain (i, p(i)+1), we need p(i) = c-1. Similarly for i'. So both holes must be at column c-1 for both rows i and i', which would mean p(i) = p(i') = c-1, but p is a permutation, so cannot have same column for two rows. So indeed a rectangle cannot contain two points of S. The proof in the solution is more convoluted but the conclusion seems correct.\n\nLemma 2: No rectangle of type B can contain two points of T. This follows by symmetry.\n\nLemma 3: No rectangle can contain a point of S and a point of T simultaneously. The proof seems correct: If rectangle contains a point (i, p(i)+1) ∈ S, then p(i) < c, so holes are left of rectangle. Then for any column j in rectangle, the hole in column j must be below rectangle (p^{-1}(j) > b). So points of T (p^{-1}(j)+1, j) have row index > b, thus cannot be inside rectangle. So rectangle cannot contain both S and T points.\n\nThus each rectangle can contain at most one point of S∪T. Since |S|≥n-1 and |T|≥n-1, we have at least 2n-2 rectangles needed. Actually they argue that |S|+|T|≥2n-2, with possible overlap at (n,n). So lower bound holds.\n\nThus the lower bound is correct.\n\nThus answer 2n-2 = 4048 is correct.\n\nNow we need to check reasoning validity: The solution uses combinatorial arguments, definitions, lemmas, proof by contradiction, explicit construction. These are valid mathematical methods. No invalid reasoning. The solution provides concrete details for the construction: defines U_k and L_k rectangles explicitly. So the construction claim is provided with explicit formulas, which is Category C (valid method). So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow check presentation issues: Are there any gaps or errors? The solution is fairly thorough. However, we need to check for any missing justification or ambiguous statements.\n\nPotential issues:\n\n- In Lemma 1 proof, they say \"Since the rectangle’s columns are a contiguous interval [c,d] with c≤p(i)+1≤d and c≤p(i')+1≤d, we have p(i)+1≥c and p(i')+1≥c.\" Actually they already have that. Then they argue \"the column p(i') (which satisfies p(i')<c≤p(i')+1) lies between the two columns p(i)+1 and p(i')+1\". This is a bit confusing but the core idea is that the rectangle contains the hole. The proof is okay but could be more concise. However, it's not a critical error.\n\n- In Lemma 3, they say \"Take any column j∈[c,d]. If the hole in column j were at a row q≤b (i.e., not below the rectangle), then (q,j) would lie in R, contradicting the fact that R contains no hole. Therefore the hole in each column of R lies below the rectangle, i.e., p^{-1}(j)>b.\" This is correct.\n\n- The lower bound argument uses |S∪T|≥|S|+|T|-1≥2n-3, then they claim stronger inequality (3) number of rectangles ≥|S|+|T|≥2n-2. They argue that at most one cell can belong to both S and T: it would have to be (n,n). Actually S includes (i,p(i)+1) for p(i)<n, so for i=n, p(n) could be n? If p(n)=n, then p(n)+1 = n+1 > n, so not in S. So S does not contain (n,n). T includes (p^{-1}(j)+1, j) for p^{-1}(j)<n, so for j=n, p^{-1}(n) could be n, then p^{-1}(n)+1 = n+1 > n, not in T. So (n,n) is not in S or T. Actually the only possible overlap is if p(i)+1 = p^{-1}(j)+1 and i = p^{-1}(j)+1? Wait S and T are sets of cells adjacent to holes: S are cells to the right of holes, T are cells below holes. Overlap would be a cell that is both to the right of some hole and below some hole. That would be a cell (i, j) such that j = p(i)+1 and i = p^{-1}(j)+1. So i = p^{-1}(p(i)+1)+1. That is possible if p(i)+1 is a column j such that the hole in column j is at row i-1. So we need p(i)+1 = j and p^{-1}(j) = i-1. That means p(i) = j-1 and p(i-1) = j? Actually p(i-1) = j? Let's solve: p(i)+1 = j, and p^{-1}(j) = i-1. So p(i-1) = j = p(i)+1. So p(i-1) = p(i)+1. So if the permutation has adjacent values differing by 1 in a certain order, there could be overlap. For example, if p(i-1) = p(i) +