e a type\u202fR rectangle \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) contains \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) and \\((i',p(i')+1)\\) with \\(i<i'\\).  \n   Condition (R) gives \\(p(i)<c\\) and \\(p(i')<c\\). Because the points are inside \\(R\\), we have \\(c\\le p(i)+1\\le d\\) and \\(c\\le p(i')+1\\le d\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   p(i)<c\\le p(i)+1,\\qquad p(i')<c\\le p(i')+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently column \\(p(i)\\) lies strictly left of the left side of \\(R\\) while column \\(p(i)+1\\) lies inside \\(R\\); the same holds for \\(i'\\). Since the rows \\(i\\) and \\(i'\\) both belong to \\([a,b]\\), the hole \\((i',p(i'))\\) would be inside \\(R\\) (its row is \\(i'\\) and its column \\(p(i')\\) satisfies \\(c\\le p(i')+1\\) and \\(p(i')<c\\)), contradicting the fact that \\(R\\) contains no hole. \u220e  \n\n   **Lemma\u202f2.** No rectangle of type\u202fB can contain two distinct points of \\(T\\).  \n   *Proof.* Apply Lemma\u202f1 to the transposed board (swap rows and columns). \u220e  \n\n   **Lemma\u202f3.** No rectangle (of either type) can contain simultaneously a point of \\(S\\) and a point of \\(T\\).  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Let \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) contain a point \\((i,p(i)+1)\\in S\\). As above, (R) forces \\(p(i)<c\\); thus every hole in a row of \\(R\\) lies left of the rectangle. Take any column \\(j\\in[c,d]\\). If the hole in column \\(j\\) were at a row \\(q\\le b\\), then \\((q,j)\\) would be inside \\(R\\), contradicting the definition of \\(R\\). Hence the hole in each column of \\(R\\) lies **below** the rectangle, i.e. \\(p^{-1}(j)>b\\). Consequently a point of the form \\((p^{-1}(j)+1,j)\\in T\\) has row index \\(p^{-1}(j)+1>b\\) and cannot belong to \\(R\\). \u220e  \n\n   From Lemmas\u202f1\u20133 each rectangle can cover **at most one** element of the set \\(S\\cup T\\).  \n   Because at most one cell can belong to both \\(S\\) and \\(T\\) (the only possible common cell is \\((n,n)\\), which occurs only when the hole in the last row coincides with the hole in